# shir_nodes/cgo_call.node — CGO-PATH expression node.
# A call into a CGO-bound native library (tree-sitter bindings, etc.)
# that has NO shell/JS/Perl semantics: the faithful translation is the
# NATIVE CALL ITSELF, executable only via the C frontend path (the
# program becomes a C-backend build linking the native library).
#
# target: dotted Go callee verbatim ("sitter.NewParser",
#         "parser.Parse", "node.Content", ...)
# args:   evaluated argument expressions
#
# Backend contract:
#   C    : renders the mapped native call (tree-sitter api.h family);
#          UNKNOWN targets render `/* cgo-path: unimplemented <target>
#          */ (assert(0), 0)` — loud, never silent.
#   JS   : sh2.cgoUnsupported("target") — throws at RUNTIME (never
#          silently wrong); browser deployments bundle the WASM/native
#          bridge instead.
#   Perl : die "otranspilerl: cgo-path construct <target> requires the C
#          frontend build\n"

node CgoCall
tag "CgoCall"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field target: string
field args: exprs
# RESOLVED — the provable half of the calling contract is the ARGUMENT LAYOUT: the
# node passes its evaluated arguments positionally, each exactly once, each occupying
# exactly its own bytes, in declaration order, with no padding and no overlap. All of
# it is proved in TextOps#cgo_call_sound:
#  * SIZE: layout args is the concatenation of the argument blobs, so its length is
#    the sum of the blob lengths (TextOps#layout_length) — no padding, no rounding, no
#    truncation;
#  * POSITION: arg_offset args k is the offset of argument k (TextOps#arg_offset_cons),
#    the buffer splits at that offset into the earlier and the later arguments
#    (TextOps#layout_take_split); dropping those bytes
#    leaves exactly the remaining arguments in order (TextOps#layout_drop_offset), and
#    taking the blob's length at that offset returns that argument's blob
#    (TextOps#layout_arg_window) — no argument's bytes are interleaved with another's;
#  * ORDER: layout is concatenation, not a set, so declaration order is load-bearing
#    (TextOps#reordered_layout_differs);
#  * NOTHING DROPPED: an empty layout means every blob was empty
#    (TextOps#layout_nil_all_blobs_nil).
# NON-VACUITY: TextOps#cgo_layout_witness checks a three-argument layout containing a
# ZERO-WIDTH argument — offsets 0, 2, 3, 3 — so a width-less argument keeps its
# position while consuming no bytes, which is exactly what a packed-by-size layout
# gets wrong; TextOps#cgo_layout_position_witness checks the window law there too.
# Three weaker models are REFUTED: padding each argument breaks the size law
# (TextOps#padding_breaks_the_size_law), laying arguments out by size reorders them
# (TextOps#reordered_layout_differs), and dropping the last argument loses its bytes
# (TextOps#dropping_an_argument_loses_its_bytes). And the layout ALONE is not the
# argument list — the same bytes are two one-byte arguments or one two-byte argument
# (TextOps#layout_alone_is_not_the_argument_list) — so the declared widths are part
# of the contract, not decoration.
# NOT PROMISED (outside this model): the C ABI type mapping beyond width, ownership
# and lifetime of returned pointers, the errno/error channel, and the call's side
# effects on process state. `result = marshal_out (C_fn (marshal_in args))` is the
# foreign function's contract, not this node's; the node only fixes that the call is
# made with those arguments, in that layout.
spec: TextOps#cgo_call_sound
